--TEST--
`Core\Cli::arguments` hands back a word that looks like syntax as the value it is — `--` ends nothing, `-abc` is one word rather than three flags and `--name=value` is one rather than a pair — spec § 15, `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`
--ARGS--
--
--
-
--name=value
-abc
;
--file
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 15's list is *raw*: nothing between the shell and this array has an opinion
// about what a word means. The existing cases pin the order, the empty program
// and the taint; what none of them reaches is the six spellings every argument
// parser in the world treats as syntax — the end-of-options `--`, the standard
// input `-`, a `name=value` pair, a bundled short cluster, a shell operator and
// a word spelled like the runner's own flag. `Core\Command::run` is where a
// meaning is assigned to any of that (`rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`), and a program reading this
// member declared no table, so it gets the words.
//
// There is exactly one word the launcher spends before the program sees it, and
// it is why this section opens with two `--` lines: a leading `--` is the
// escape token `nvs run`'s own parser consumes, the way `cargo run --` does,
// and it is consumed once and only in that position. The second one is the
// program's, which is what the first expected element asserts.
//
// Asserted by **counting** rather than off a line: a member that consumed `--`
// or split `-abc` still prints a plausible list, and fails both counts here.
array<string> $expected = ["--", "-", "--name=value", "-abc", ";", "--file"];
array<tainted string> $words = Core\Cli::arguments();

uint $at = 0;
uint $identical = 0;
uint $characters = 0;
foreach ($words as tainted string $word) {
    if ($at < Core\Arr::count($expected) && $word == $expected[$at]) {
        $identical = $identical + 1;
    }
    $characters = $characters + Core\Str::length($word);
    $at = $at + 1;
}

// Three independent counts, because each fails to a different mistake: the
// first to a word consumed or invented, the second to one reordered or altered,
// the third to one silently split — a split keeps every character while the
// count of words it arrives in changes, and a `--` eaten as a separator changes
// the characters while leaving five plausible words behind.
echo "words=", Core\Arr::count($words), "\n";
echo "identical=", $identical, "\n";
echo "characters=", $characters, "\n";
--EXPECT--
words=6
identical=6
characters=26
